 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283003 13194139534245 true] [17592186282999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283004 13194139534245 true] [17592186282999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534245 true] [17592186282999 1041 "selection" 13194139534245 true] [17592186282999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283001 13194139534245 true] [17592186282999 1110 #uuid "746eb990-d795-58ac-b486-d547ad6ba17a" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283000 1034 "-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283000 1111 #uuid "3003d2c6-3b38-5e95-b719-5b9a0d5c9642" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283001 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283001 1111 #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283002 1034 "1" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283002 1111 #uuid "dab393c9-b76c-5660-bb79-30c2d41d6439" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283003 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283003 1111 #uuid "e84b7c88-92b9-57ba-a71c-0fc867044a59" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283004 1034 "\\frac{7}{3}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283004 1111 #uuid "58eefba0-9a07-54df-9842-45ae83596040" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283006 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1089 "q-15646" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{5}$ and the point $P(2,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1113 #uuid "2d6fc4e8-faa4-5439-8ad8-f1b03b3d2be1" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283005 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283007 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283008 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283009 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283010 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283011 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1041 "selection" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283011 13194139534245 true] [17592186283006 1110 #uuid "4f7a5989-e6d1-5515-b407-8df842245c56" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283007 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283007 1111 #uuid "b1aaed71-57db-59c5-ab3c-c1667fd06692" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283008 1034 "2" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283008 1111 #uuid "73cb4b26-e2b5-5bba-b128-7e87e156618d" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283009 1034 "1" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283009 1111 #uuid "9b1a7af8-21b1-5e1a-8c0c-e73e0647e4a9" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283010 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283010 1111 #uuid "b96377cf-d0db-5d3a-b091-b65ef9f6fa37" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283011 1034 "5" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283011 1111 #uuid "2fc7c712-f332-5322-aa66-499961a595aa" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283013 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1089 "q-342589" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1100 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1113 #uuid "ec34106c-1275-5721-882b-cb32e2aa950e" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283012 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283014 13194139534245 true] [17592186283013 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534245 true] [17592186283013 1041 "field-1" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283013 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283014 13194139534245 true] [17592186283013 1110 #uuid "071430b0-ac1c-5c5d-bfb0-7aacd0d96541" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283014 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283014 1111 #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283016 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1089 "q-342609" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $0\\le x<2,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1113 #uuid "203d422f-9b1d-5a14-a3ec-68b4a7ed16ff" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283015 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $0\\le x<2,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}-4(1)}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283017 13194139534245 true] [17592186283016 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534245 true] [17592186283016 1041 "field-1" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283016 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283017 13194139534245 true] [17592186283016 1110 #uuid "894baeef-f0ad-5392-955d-aa043b1917b3" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283017 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534245 true] [17592186283017 1111 #uuid "f0eec1c4-c10a-532c-a5fa-9e263ee5696a" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283019 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1089 "q-75987" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1113 #uuid "d8c7f142-6d6f-5e69-8355-8be41cf102c8" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283018 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283020 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283021 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283022 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283023 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283024 13194139534245 true] [17592186283019 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534245 true] [175921